## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer data structures, concurrency, and practical OS scheduling under contention.

## Key Concepts
- Timer wheel: bucketed deadlines for O(1) insert
- Concurrency: per-bucket locking or lock-free heads
- Expiration path: wake task, reschedule, handle cancellations

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Timer {
  uint64_t expiry; // system ticks
  struct Task* t;
  struct Timer* next;
} Timer;

#define WHEEL_SIZE 256

typedef struct TimerWheel {
  Timer* buckets[WHEEL_SIZE];
  uint64_t current;
  spinlock_t lock;
} TimerWheel;

void insert_timer(TimerWheel* w, Timer* tm, uint64_t expiry) {
  uint64_t now = get_current_tick();
  uint64_t delta = (expiry > now) ? (expiry - now) : 0;
  uint64_t idx = (w->current + (delta % WHEEL_SIZE)) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  tm->expiry = expiry;
  tm->next = w->buckets[idx];
  w->buckets[idx] = tm;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you cancel a timer already in the wheel?
- How does this scale with multiple CPUs and large timer counts?
- How would you handle tick drift or long sleeps?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about live-code updates in a running kernel, including synchronization, cache coherence, and control of instruction fetch paths under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Live patching of executable memory without stopping workers
- Per-page state machines and atomic publish
- I-cache/TLB synchronization and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal C-like sketch (pseudo-code)
enum { STABLE, STAGED, ACTIVE } state_t;
typedef struct { void *addr; void *code; state_t s; volatile int refcnt; uint64_t ver; } page_t;

int publish_patch(page_t *p, void *new_code) {
  p->s = STAGED;
  p->code = new_code;
  __sync_synchronize();
  if (atomic_compare_exchange(&p->s, STAGED, ACTIVE)) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&p->ver, 1);
    icache_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    tlb_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    return 0;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you guarantee rollback if a patch proves faulty?
- How to test latency under burst patch arrivals and mixed workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives and common OS patterns, especially producer-consumer, deadlock avoidance, and correct semaphore/mutex usage in C.

## Key Concepts
- Semaphores
- Mutex
- Circular buffer
- Deadlock detection
- Boundary conditions (full/empty)

## Code Example
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define N 10

int buffer[N];
int in = 0, out = 0;
sem_t empty, full;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void produce(int item) {
  sem_wait(&empty);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  buffer[in] = item;
  in = (in + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&full);
}

int consume() {
  int item;
  sem_wait(&full);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  item = buffer[out];
  out = (out + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&empty);
  return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers and ensure fairness?
- What are trade-offs vs. using condition variables instead of semaphores?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical NUMA-aware memory management, race-free migration, and measurement under contention.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA locality
- Page migration and pte updates
- Atomic synchronization and CAS
- TLB shootdown and cache coherence
- Contention control and testing

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int node;
  atomic_int heat;
  atomic_bool migrating;
} page_meta_t;

void try_migrate(page_t *p, int new_node) {
  if (atomic_load(&p->migrating)) return;
  if (atomic_exchange(&p->migrating, true)) return;
  // update pte, migrate data to new_node
  atomic_store(&p->node, new_node);
  // flush/invalidate as needed
  atomic_store(&p->migrating, false);
}
```

