## Why This Is Asked

Understanding safe concurrent data sharing is fundamental for OS-level IPC. This task tests practical use of atomics, memory ordering, and race testing.

## Key Concepts

- Ring buffer basics, head/tail invariants
- Atomic operations: CAS, fetch_add, acquire/release
- Memory ordering and visibility on multi-core CPUs
- Overflow handling and backpressure

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t head;
  _Atomic uint32_t tail;
  int slots[256];
} ring_t;

bool enqueue(ring_t *r, int val){
  uint32_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_acquire);
  if (t - h >= 256) return false; // full
  r->slots[t & 255] = val;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(ring_t *r, int *out){
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&r->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return false; // empty
  *out = r->slots[h & 255];
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->head, 1, memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to multiple consumers?
- How would you mitigate false sharing and cache contention?
- How would you test liveness and bounded-buffer guarantees?

## Why This Is Asked

Beginners should reason about virtual memory protection, per-thread isolation, and fault handling without requiring full OS support. Guard pages are a simple, practical mechanism to detect overflows early.

## Key Concepts

- Virtual memory protection and per-thread stacks
- Guard page placement and page faults
- Graceful thread termination and resource cleanup
- Basic testing for concurrency and fault paths

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal skeleton
typedef struct { void *stack; void *guard; } Thread;

void thread_init(Thread *t) {
  t->stack = mmap(NULL, 8*1024*1024, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
                  MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
  t->guard = (char*)t->stack - PAGE_SIZE;
  mprotect(t->guard, PAGE_SIZE, PROT_NONE);
}

void fault_handler(void *addr, Thread *t) {
  if (addr >= t->guard && addr < (char*)t->guard + PAGE_SIZE) {
    terminate_thread(t);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to optionally grow the stack on demand?
- What testing strategy ensures no collateral damage when multiple threads fault simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
Examines a practical sleep-wake path and basic data-structuring under concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Timer wheel with fixed slots for constant-time operations
- Safe concurrent enqueue and wakeup via spinlocks
- Tick progression and wrap-around handling

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct Thread { int id; struct Thread *next; } Thread;

#define WHEEL_SIZE 64
typedef struct {
  Thread *buckets[WHEEL_SIZE];
  int current_tick;
  SpinLock lock;
} TimerWheel;

void sleep_ms(Thread *t, int ms, TimerWheel *w) {
  int slot = (w->current_tick + ms) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  t->next = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = t;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
}

void timer_tick(TimerWheel *w) {
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  int slot = w->current_tick % WHEEL_SIZE;
  Thread *cur = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = NULL;
  w->current_tick++;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
  while (cur) { wake(cur); cur = cur->next; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for correctness under concurrent enqueues and wakes?
- What changes if ms can exceed wheel size multiple times?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests container-level CPU bandwidth enforcement and fairness under contention, requiring concrete accounting, throttling paths, and incremental patching.

## Key Concepts

- per-container quotas
- credits accounting
- burst windows and throttling
- starvation testing and correctness under contention

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo patch sketch for per-container credit enforcement
typedef struct {
  int container_id;
  unsigned long rem_credits;
} task_t;

void schedule_tick(task_t *t, unsigned long run_ns) {
  if (t->rem_credits < run_ns) {
    throttle(t); // yield until credits recharge
  } else {
    t->rem_credits -= run_ns;
    run_task(t);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate realistic bursts and idle periods to validate the recharge model?
- How would you extend to nested cgroups and multi-tenant fairness guarantees?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical design of per-CPU memory caches and cross-CPU coherence under contention, emphasizing low latency and minimal traffic.

## Key Concepts
- Per-CPU caches
- Eviction policies (LRU, hotness)
- Ownership migration and races
- Concurrency control and perf measurement

## Code Example
```c
// compact victim selection sketch
static struct page *select_victim(struct cpu_cache *cc) {
    // iterate LRU list, skip pinned pages, pick first unreferenced
    struct page *p = cc->lru_tail; // tail is oldest
    while (p && is_pinned(p)) p = p->prev;
    return p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would NUMA affect this design? How would you adapt the policy?
- How would you validate reduced cross-CPU traffic with measurements?

Why This Is Asked: tests understanding of synchronization primitives, fairness, and memory ordering. Key Concepts: ticket locks, wake queues, avoiding starvation, spurious wakeups, memory barriers. Code Example: see below. Follow-up: discuss adaptation to multicore/NUMA, impact of preemption, and testing strategies.

Code Example:
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>

typedef struct {
  atomic_uint next;
  atomic_uint nowServing;
} ticket_lock_t;

void ticket_lock_init(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  atomic_store(&lk->next, 0);
  atomic_store(&lk->nowServing, 0);
}

unsigned ticket_lock_acquire(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  unsigned ticket = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lk->next, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (atomic_load_explicit(&lk->nowServing, memory_order_acquire) != ticket) {
    _mm_pause();
  }
  return ticket;
}

void ticket_lock_release(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lk->nowServing, 1, memory_order_release);
}
```

Follow-up Questions:
- How would you test fairness under heavy contention?
- How would you extend to multiple locks and prevent deadlock?

