## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of probabilistic scheduling, fairness, and simple kernel-like data paths without overwhelming complexity.

## Key Concepts
- Lottery scheduling basics; per-process tickets; cumulative distribution; random number generation; blocking/wake interactions.
- Data structures: ready list with ticket counts; total_tickets; small RNG; race-free updates.
- Testing: compare empirical waiting time and starvation under IO-bound vs CPU-bound mixes.

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  int pid;
  int tickets;
  int state; // READY/BLOCKED
} proc_t;

static uint32_t rng = 12345;
static uint32_t lcg(void){ rng = 1664525u*rng + 1013904223u; return rng; }

proc_t* select_next(proc_t** procs, int n){
  int total = 0; for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(procs[i]->state==READY) total += procs[i]->tickets;
  if(total<=0) return NULL;
  int r = lcg() % total + 1;
  int acc = 0; for(int i=0;i<n;i++) if(procs[i]->state==READY){ acc += procs[i]->tickets; if(acc >= r) return procs[i]; }
  return NULL;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple CPUs and true parallel dispatch?
- How would you prevent ticket manipulation by a misbehaving process and ensure numerical robustness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests lock-free IPC, per-service quotas, and NUMA-aware memory placement under high contention. It also probes correctness guarantees, memory visibility, and testing strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free data structures
- Per-service quotas and throttling
- NUMA-aware memory placement
- Correctness under preemption and cache coherence

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal enqueue sketch (pseudo C)
struct Slot { uint64_t seq; char data[DATA_SZ]; };
volatile struct Slot *ring; // mapped
size_t head = 0; // per-producer index
size_t tail = 0; // consumer index
void enqueue(void *src){
  uint64_t s = fetch_add(&ring[head].seq, 1);
  // write data
  memcpy(ring[head].data, src, DATA_SZ);
  // publish
  ring[head].seq = s + 1;
  head = (head + 1) % RING_SIZE;
}
void dequeue(void *dst){
  uint64_t s = ring[tail].seq;
  if (s == expected){
    memcpy(dst, ring[tail].data, DATA_SZ);
    ring[tail].seq = s + 1;
    tail = (tail + 1) % RING_SIZE;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate fairness under bursty workloads?
- How would you adapt the design for bulk transfer of variable-sized messages?

